diagnosis_text:
Router4 is up and functioning normally. The LAN gateway interface (router4 eth0, 192.168.200.1/24) is UP and responding to hosts. From each affected PC (pc11–pc14), pings to the default gateway 192.168.200.1 succeed with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs. Those same hosts can also reach external subnets: pc11–pc14 successfully ping 172.16.10.4 (pc4) offsite, proving routing “out of the room” works.

On router4 itself, eth0 (192.168.200.1), the uplinks eth4 (192.168.15.2) and eth5 (172.25.1.1) are UP. The routing table is populated (default via 172.25.1.2 and multiple RIP-learned prefixes), and router4 can ping its upstream neighbor (router3 at 10.50.1.1) and remote networks (e.g., 172.16.10.4) directly. These facts contradict the report of a dead gateway.

Localization: No failure observed on router4 or the local switch segment for 192.168.200.0/24. All relevant interfaces are operational, and end-to-end reachability from Location C to at least one remote site is confirmed.

Root cause: No network or router fault present; the outage report appears to be a misdiagnosis or a transient/host-side issue.

Corrective fix: No action on router4 required. Advise users to recheck host firewalls/DNS or application issues; continue monitoring. If the problem recurs, collect timestamps and specific destinations to correlate and investigate further. 
